#N canvas 40 60 1280 1120 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 18\ \ \ Self-organizing\ maps\ \ -\ \ from\ a\ codebook\ to\ a\ map\ of\ timbres\ (Kohonen\ 1982) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1090 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1090 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1090 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 No teacher and no reward: 36 units only see examples and organize themselves. Each one holds a point of a timbre plane (brightness \, pitch). Competing for the examples they become a codebook \, pulling their neighbours along they become a MAP - neighbours on the grid sound alike., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 780 empty empty THE\ IDEA:\ learning\ without\ a\ teacher 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 Lessons 4 to 6 learned from answers \, lesson 17 from rewards. Here nothing tells the units what is right. They see examples - points of the timbre plane: x = brightness \, y = pitch - and each unit holds a point of its own \, its weight vector w (the coloured dots)., f 52;
#X text 20 218 COMPETITION (Rojas \, chapter 5): for every example the unit nearest to it wins \, and only the winner learns:, f 52;
#X text 30 269 w <- w + eta * (x - w), f 52;
#X text 20 290 This is online k-means. The 36 winners become a CODEBOOK: 36 prototypes that stand for every point of the shape. Sending only the number of the nearest prototype is vector quantization - how images and voices were compressed in the 1980s and 90s., f 52;
#X text 20 386 NEIGHBOURS (Kohonen \, 1982): the units also sit on a grid. The winner pulls its grid neighbours along - every unit within the radius r on the grid learns too. Neighbours on the grid end up near each other in the plane: a MAP that keeps the topology. Radius 0 = plain competition \, no map., f 52;
#X text 20 497 THE SCHEDULE: start with a big radius and eta - the map unfolds as a whole - then shrink both so that each unit finds its own place. Shrink too fast and a knot freezes in., f 52;
#X text 20 563 THE CORTEX DOES THIS: the visual field and the skin are mapped onto the cortex with neighbours kept together \, a flat cortex folding to map a richer world gives the stripes of ocular dominance (Rojas \, chapter 15)., f 52;
#X text 20 644 IN MUSIC: Kohonen maps have charted the keys of tonal music (Leman \, 1990s) and timbre spaces (Toiviainen \, 1998). Here walking the map plays a smooth path through the sounds it learned., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 860 470 empty empty THE\ MAP\ \ (dots\ =\ units\,\ coloured\ by\ their\ place\ on\ the\ grid) 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
