#X obj 10 886 cnv 1 1260 200 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 30 912 1) Press train (grid \, square): the scrambled dots unfold into an ordered grid - the colours stay in order and the topographic error falls to about 0%. Then walk the map: neighbouring notes are neighbouring timbres., f 56;
#X text 30 993 2) Radius 0 \, scramble \, train: a codebook without order. It covers the square just as well \, but the walk jumps around and the topographic error is around 80%., f 56;
#X text 446 912 3) Chain: the 36 units become a line that fills the square like a space-filling (Peano) curve - Rojas \, exercise 15.1. Shape "line": the chain lays itself out in order along it., f 56;
#X text 446 978 4) Islands and plus: the map has to stretch across empty space - some units end up where no example ever falls and never win again: dead units., f 56;
#X text 862 912 5) Glide the probe: this is vector quantization. A gesture becomes a few codewords., f 56;
#X text 862 948 6) Scramble \, radius 1 \, learn live: the map can tie itself in a knot and stay knotted (a metastable state \, Rojas 15.3.4). A big radius first unties it., f 56;
